# ==================================================================
# LINE ENDINGS, AND THE ONE PLACE THEY ARE LOAD-BEARING
# ==================================================================
# Normalize to LF in the repository; check out native on Windows. That is
# the default below, and it is fine for source, because nothing compares a
# .dart or .md file to bytes produced in-process.
#
# IT IS NOT FINE FOR A BYTE-EXACT GOLDEN. CR-117 is `write(read(bytes)) ==
# bytes`, asserted on real files read off the disk they ship on — the four
# starter projects and the codec fixtures. A file committed as LF and
# checked out as CRLF is a different file, so the byte-exact suite fails
# on a clean clone while passing for everyone who already had the tree.
# That failure has already happened once during this stage.
#
# So every byte-exact-tested artifact gets an explicit `eol=lf`, and
# `apps/rackpal/test/gitattributes_byte_exactness_test.dart` FAILS if one
# of them is not covered here. THE TEST IS THE MECHANISM; this list is
# just where the answer is written down. Adding a fixture with a new
# extension and forgetting this file is a red suite, not a surprise on
# somebody else's machine six weeks later.
#
# WHY `eol=lf` AND NOT `binary` FOR THE GOLDENS: `binary` (= `-text -diff
# -merge`) would guarantee byte-exactness unconditionally, but it also
# turns off `git diff`. CR-091 makes `.rackpal` plain, pretty-printed,
# key-sorted UTF-8 JSON precisely so a change to one is readable, and the
# four starters are goldens whose diff IS the review — `binary` would
# reduce a regenerated starter to "Bin 812345 -> 812901 bytes". `eol=lf`
# keeps the diff and is exactly as safe here: the canonical writer escapes
# control characters, so a `.rackpal` never contains a raw CR for
# normalisation to touch, and the suite asserts that too rather than
# assuming it.
* text=auto

*.dart   text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.ps1    text eol=crlf
*.bat    text eol=crlf

# BYTE-EXACT GOLDENS — read the block above before changing these.
# `.rppj` is a project (CR-091, renamed by DR-015 and again by DR-179);
# `.rpdx` is a single device template in the same JSON regime (CR-063);
# `.rpda` is the device archive DR-015 named and DR-100 shaped.
#
# EVERY SPELLING A BUILD HAS WRITTEN STAYS LISTED (DR-179 §11.178.3). The
# app still opens `.rmpj`, `.rmdx`, `.rmda` and the pre-DR-015 `.rackmate`,
# and still saves each in place, so one arriving through a checkout must
# survive byte-for-byte exactly as before; dropping the line the day the
# extension changed would corrupt precisely the files the back-compat path
# exists to protect.
#
# `.rackpal` IS NOT LISTED AND NEVER SHOULD HAVE BEEN. The product rename
# put it here in `0e2c5bb` in place of `.rackmate`, and no build has ever
# written one (DR-179 §11.178.1). `.rmdev` is listed from DR-015's day,
# when it was a specified name nothing wrote; it costs nothing and stays.
*.rppj      text eol=lf
*.rpdx      text eol=lf
*.rpda      text eol=lf
*.rmpj      text eol=lf
*.rmdx      text eol=lf
*.rmda      text eol=lf
*.rackmate  text eol=lf
*.rmdev     text eol=lf

*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.ico    binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.pdf    binary

# ==================================================================
# VENDORED NATIVE BINARIES
# ==================================================================
# A DLL rewritten by EOL normalisation is a corrupt DLL. `text=auto`
# would in practice detect it as binary and leave it alone, which is a
# guess about content rather than a rule about this path, and CR-100 put
# the binary in the tree deliberately enough to say so.
third_party/pdfium/** binary

# The committed lockfile (DR-248 section 11.247.5). pub writes it with LF; a
# CRLF checkout invites a rewrite on the first `pub get`, so check it out
# the way pub writes it.
pubspec.lock text eol=lf

# ==================================================================
# THE macOS RUNNER (DR-009, DR-256)
# ==================================================================
# The macOS CI job checks out LF, and Xcode and plutil expect LF in a
# .pbxproj, .xcscheme, .plist, .entitlements or .xcconfig. The root
# `* text=auto` would give a Windows checkout CRLF in all of them, so the
# whole tree is pinned `eol=lf` and a Mac and a Windows checkout read the
# same bytes. The rasters are listed AFTER it because a later line wins:
# `text eol=lf` would otherwise re-enable text handling that the earlier
# `*.png binary` line had switched off.
apps/rackpal/macos/** text eol=lf
apps/rackpal/macos/**/*.png binary
apps/rackpal/macos/**/*.icns binary
